1
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:11,160
Turn on the lights and try moving away from the room,
will you?

2
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,700
Surely we can manage this place somehow.

3
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:47,980
You understand, right?
Yeah, yeah, I understand.

4
00:00:48,270 --> 00:00:51,900
Once you get it,
just keep quiet and follow me.

5
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:17,420
Hana-chan.

6
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,980
It's not Hana-chan after all.

7
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:28,920
Junko.

8
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:38,280
How ridiculous.

9
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,700
What do you think you're doing?

10
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,320
It said right there that it would continue after the commercial break,
didn't it?

11
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:46,760
It's just next week's preview, anyway.

12
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,500
So that was the talked-about
love stick dance?

13
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:50,800
I watched it for the first time, though.

14
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,440
It was interesting, wasn't it?

15
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:53,460
Boring.

16
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:56,100
That can't be right.

17
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:59,400
It's the highest-rated
drama on TV right now.

18
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,020
They're filming this one
with a 30% rating every week.

19
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,520
Just because the ratings are high,

20
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,420
that doesn't mean it's interesting,
that's why.

21
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:09,180
Welcome back, Yukata Brothers of the Valley.

22
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:18,320
Just how exactly are TV
ratings decided, anyway?

23
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,980
W-well, I'm sure it's from some
kind of survey or something.

24
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:25,440
Nobody's ever given me a survey,
I'll tell you that.

25
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,580
Have you ever been surveyed?

26
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:31,240
Maybe there's a device inside
the TV that checks the ratings.

27
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:34,660
I've never seen or heard of a TV like that.

28
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,960
Isn't the media just
deciding them on its own?

29
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,040
N-no way.

30
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,600
Besides,
take that Love Bon Odori dance from earlier.

31
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:45,000
You're the only one who'd watch
something like that this much.

32
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:45,960
W-what...

33
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:49,040
I was watching it because they
said it was a high-ratings drama.

34
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:51,460
I was trying not to be late for the trip.

35
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:55,160
Typical Japanese,
getting swept up in every trend.

36
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:55,940
Yeah.

37
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:59,260
Ryotsu, let's play a game to kill some time.

38
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:01,320
Yeah, we played plenty of them earlier,
didn't we?

39
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:03,440
Rather than that,
why don't we watch a video?

40
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:06,980
Actually,
I got my hands on an amazing foreign video.

41
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:08,500
A foreign video?

42
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:11,800
It's about setting up
a camp on managed land.

43
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,760
It's an educational video
from the US Marine Corps.

44
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,540
And it's really well made.

45
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,700
What's so interesting about that video?

46
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:21,320
Then how about this one?

47
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:24,040
It's a video on precautions
for a desert march,

48
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,880
so bring a better video.

49
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:30,220
Right, if it's videos,
I've got plenty of kitchen knives.

50
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:31,200
Kitchen knives.

51
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:32,380
Who's that?

52
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,640
...lives in the neighborhood, you know.
Don't you know him?

53
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:44,700
Apparently he's a senior I have no memory of at all.
Hojo, a Tokyo University grad, volunteered

54
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:51,020
to work at a police box. A Tokyo U grad,
so he must have a really sharp-looking face.

55
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:54,400
I bet he does.
Ohh, he's quite the one, isn't he?

56
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:56,520
Yeah, yeah.

57
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:01,880
Why was Hojo sleeping?

58
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:02,620
Yes.

59
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:05,180
This guy is Hojo?

60
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,540
He doesn't look like a
Tokyo University elite at all.

61
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:10,800
What's your business, Ryo-san?

62
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:11,360
What?

63
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,240
Do I know him?

64
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:13,800
Yes.

65
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:15,980
Senior Constable Kankichi Ryotsu

66
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:17,720
Stationed at Kameari Kōen-mae Police Box

67
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,300
Born in Shibamata, Katsushika Ward, Tokyo

68
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:22,840
Ginji Ryotsu,
who runs Tenyorozeya in Tsukuda

69
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:24,480
Born as the eldest son of Yone

70
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:27,100
Two brothers; the younger,
Kinjiro, is a lawyer

71
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,800
Kinjiro's wife Keiko's family home

72
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:38,980
Why do you know so much about that?
If it's about people living in this dormitory, everyone knows this much.

73
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:48,820
Not everyone. It's Officer Makoto Muranaka in Room 1 on the first floor, who belongs to the Katsushika Traffic Squad.
No need to say that.

74
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:58,940
I heard before that this guy has an amazing memory.
No, I just remember things I've seen or heard once.

75
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:01,160
As expected from Tokyo University.

76
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:05,480
Videos? I own about 512 of them.

77
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:08,400
Quite a lot, isn't it?

78
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:12,100
But they all look like serious videos.

79
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:14,780
Isn't there something more fun?

80
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:16,140
Well, I can't really say that...

81
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:17,880
Hold on...

82
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:21,920
What's recorded isn't
necessarily what the label says.

83
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:26,760
So they make the labels look dull
and stuff something else inside?

84
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:27,140
Phew!

85
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:29,600
Phew, the serious ones are always

86
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,560
the ones collecting the
most perverted videos.

87
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:33,720
I-is that so...

88
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:35,140
Alright, let's check!

89
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:42,520
The 20th century was a century of war.

90
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,440
Exactly what it says on the label.

91
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:45,420
W-what about the others?

92
00:05:49,220 --> 00:05:52,760
Yes, starting today, we'll be
presenting it as a three-part series.

93
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:53,260
Next!

94
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:54,640
Come on, next!

95
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:54,780
Next!

96
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:56,460
Next!

97
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:58,400
Next!

98
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:04,300
All of them still have their labels,
so the contents are unknown.

99
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:07,000
What a dull, uninteresting guy.

100
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:07,940
Sorry.

101
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:13,820
What's that machine under the deck?

102
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:14,960
Th-that is...

103
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:15,600
That is?

104
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:18,220
What is it, Hojo?

105
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:20,180
N-no, it's nothing.

106
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:23,180
As if it's nothing!
What kind of machine is it?

107
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:25,660
Um...

108
00:06:26,452 --> 00:06:29,820
So it's a machine you
can't tell people about,

109
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:33,060
don't tell me it's a machine
that removes the censor blur!

110
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:33,760
What?

111
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:35,780
The censor blur disappears?

112
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:39,382
N-no, it's not that,

113
00:06:39,432 --> 00:06:42,060
it's not that kind of machine.

114
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:43,340
Then what is it?

115
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:44,660
W-well, that's...

116
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:48,560
Factory-kun,
if you're really not going to tell us,

117
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:50,920
we've got our own ways of making you talk.

118
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:54,300
Please, stop.

119
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,360
That laughter is kind of scary, too.

120
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:07,940
If you want me to stop,
come clean and confess.

121
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:09,760
I'll tell you.

122
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:11,340
Really?

123
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:12,380
Yes.

124
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:14,920
Okay, stop.

125
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:25,280
Uuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu...

126
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:29,140
Actually, this is a meter
device that measures TV ratings.

127
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,080
What?

128
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:34,040
So this is how they were
checking the ratings?

129
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:38,440
A person from a ratings research company
came to visit me just the other day.

130
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:41,120
I'm from this company, so...

131
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:46,220
Your household has been chosen

132
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:48,920
as a sample for the ratings survey.

133
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:51,200
Would you mind cooperating with the survey?

134
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:54,560
But why on earth you, of all people?

135
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:57,640
You're not being scammed by some fake sales pitch,
are you?

136
00:07:58,120 --> 00:07:59,020
Not at all!

137
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:01,920
They don't charge money;
they even pay us a reward.

138
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:05,220
What channel you watched, and at what time,

139
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:06,740
gets recorded by this device,

140
00:08:06,740 --> 00:08:08,340
and the radio waves coming out of here

141
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:10,000
are picked up by this machine over here,

142
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,300
and sent to the survey
company through the phone line.

143
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,500
So that's how the gadget works.

144
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:20,080
What I just told you is a secret, okay?

145
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:23,160
We were told to keep it secret
that the meter device is attached.

146
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:25,340
We were strictly warned about it.

147
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:26,400
Why?

148
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:29,960
Isn't it because if someone finds out,
it could be exploited?

149
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,600
I hear media people are
excluded from the sample.

150
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:38,780
So that means,
from the media's point of view,

151
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:42,380
it's a device they desperately want.

152
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:47,520
Hojo, you're going to cooperate with us.

153
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:49,400
N-no way.

154
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:52,140
You're not allowed to
say you won't cooperate!

155
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:10,020
Mandai-chan, how've you been?

156
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:11,900
Y-yes.

157
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:13,860
Let's go drinking again sometime.

158
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:17,180
Yo, Gyotsu-chan, long time no see.

159
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:20,040
Hello, th-this is

160
00:09:20,040 --> 00:09:22,060
a producer I know.

161
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:24,100
So, what's up today?

162
00:09:24,480 --> 00:09:26,640
Did you bring some interesting project?

163
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:29,560
Something that'll rake in ratings, you know.

164
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:29,980
Yeah.

165
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:33,460
Actually, I came to sell ratings.

166
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,260
Sell ratings?

167
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:44,020
I think he'd be interested in this too.

168
00:09:47,780 --> 00:09:49,640
We can't talk about this here.

169
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:50,580
Let's go further in.

170
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:54,500
How did you end up with something like this?

171
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,900
This is a ratings meter, isn't it?

172
00:09:57,900 --> 00:09:59,380
Well, you know your stuff.

173
00:09:59,380 --> 00:10:03,740
Actually, our household was
picked for the ratings survey.

174
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:04,600
And?

175
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:08,260
And, well, I've never actually
seen one in person either.

176
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:09,680
It must be valuable, right?

177
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:12,510
Yeah, even people in the industry,

178
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:14,760
no, precisely because we're in the industry,

179
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:17,140
we hardly ever get to see one.

180
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:21,760
About 6,000 households nationwide
are part of the ratings survey,

181
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:26,460
and even in the Kanto area,
there are 15.59 million households with TVs.

182
00:10:26,940 --> 00:10:30,020
Only 600 of those have one of these meters.

183
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:33,400
What? Only 600 out of 15.59 million?

184
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:37,560
It's one for every 25,983 households, right?

185
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:40,540
So can they get accurate figures from that?

186
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:42,940
Apparently, statistically it's fine.

187
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:47,760
Still, the fact that they produce
ratings from just 600 units means

188
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:49,440
each one of these

189
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:51,700
represents about 0.17%.

190
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:53,120
That's less than I expected.

191
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:59,820
Only 0.17%,
but it should be valuable to TV stations.

192
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:00,580
Right?

193
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:09,620
That's rough. Come to think of it, didn't a show I used to
handle get canceled because its ratings didn't reach 10%?

194
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:13,020
That's right. It got up to 9.9%.

195
00:11:13,820 --> 00:11:20,460
Just 0.1% more. If one person in the sample households had watched,
it would've hit 10%.

196
00:11:20,460 --> 00:11:23,500
See, that's when this thing comes in handy.

197
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,520
How about 10,000 yen per time?

198
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:28,400
No way, no way.

199
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:31,280
I can't possibly resort
to a trick like this.

200
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:33,540
Don't you want good ratings?

201
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,660
Of course we want good ratings.

202
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:40,000
We want them so badly it hurts.

203
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:44,440
But even so,
we wouldn't go so far as to cheat for them.

204
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:48,920
We can't sell our souls as TV men.

205
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:58,480
Because we work hard every day to raise our ratings.
If we pulled a stunt like that, how could we face the staff who are doing their best every day?

206
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:02,420
Effort. Yes,
take the early evening news, for example.

207
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:10,580
Back in the day, the evening news was always set for six o'clock.
But the content of the news is pretty much the same everywhere.

208
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:16,180
So one station thought that if it started a little earlier,
its ratings might go up.

209
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:18,740
It moved its program's start time earlier.

210
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:23,580
Seeing this, the other stations
moved their start times up as well.

211
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:27,540
That's how the news start times
kept getting earlier and earlier.

212
00:12:29,020 --> 00:12:31,500
All just to squeeze out
a little more viewership.

213
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:35,200
I see, so that's the reason behind it.

214
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:38,660
Before long,
won't it merge with the lunchtime news?

215
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:46,260
Efforts to raise ratings are being made everywhere.
Recently, you often see subtitles on variety shows, right?

216
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:49,360
Subtitles? Like the ones that have been
showing up on screen this whole time.

217
00:12:52,600 --> 00:13:00,400
This tells viewers where the laugh is. If they see the subtitles,
they can laugh with confidence.

218
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:04,220
That's what boosts the ratings. I see.

219
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:07,660
And to keep viewers
interested the whole time,

220
00:13:08,140 --> 00:13:10,200
they also put subtitles here.

221
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:12,800
Am I supposed to do something?

222
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:15,740
The most important spot is
right before the commercial.

223
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:18,060
To keep viewers from leaving,

224
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:20,160
they show highlight scenes
from the second half.

225
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:21,720
Like this:

226
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:24,660
"Coming up next,
an unbelievable turn of events!"

227
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:26,560
That feels good.

228
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:29,780
You've seen lots of subtitles
on variety shows lately, right?

229
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:30,800
Subtitles?

230
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:32,860
Look, they've been
showing up this whole time.

231
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:39,140
These tell viewers
where the funny parts are.

232
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:42,620
Viewers can relax and laugh
when they follow the subtitles.

233
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:45,240
And that's what boosts the ratings.

234
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:46,320
I see.

235
00:13:47,780 --> 00:13:50,080
Aren't we having the same
conversation as before?

236
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:52,900
Yes, it's exactly the same as
before the commercial break.

237
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:55,840
The only difference is the subtitles here.

238
00:13:56,260 --> 00:13:58,260
So that even people who tune
in partway through can follow.

239
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:00,840
They include a bit of the earlier scenes.

240
00:14:01,340 --> 00:14:04,100
Viewers these days switch
channels so quickly.

241
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:06,380
You really go through a lot to make these shows,
don't you?

242
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:08,740
What hard work?

243
00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:12,060
You're just pulling viewers along with all these cheap tricks,
damn it.

244
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:13,340
That's uncalled for.

245
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:17,620
Ratings are the only
fair way to judge a show.

246
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:22,040
Working hard to raise them
is only natural for a TV man.

247
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:26,200
But does all that effort
really show up in the ratings?

248
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:28,680
Of course it does.

249
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:34,580
But if every other show copied it at once,
the boost would disappear.

250
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:38,100
Then we'd just come up
with another approach.

251
00:14:38,480 --> 00:14:40,960
You don't need such cheap tricks

252
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:47,060
when there's a sure chance here
to raise the ratings by 0.17%!

253
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:50,260
No, no, no.

254
00:14:50,260 --> 00:14:54,000
No matter how badly we want ratings,
we absolutely can't do that.

255
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:02,660
Hojo, do you know which ratings
survey company left this device here?

256
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:05,180
Yes, they gave me a business card.

257
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:07,800
It's a company called Nakagawa Research.

258
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:10,540
Nakagawa? Then could it be...

259
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:11,038
Yes.

260
00:15:11,088 --> 00:15:15,840
It's one of the companies run by Officer Keiichi Nakagawa,
who works at the Kameari Kōen-mae police box.

261
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,620
Hoho, that makes it even more convenient.

262
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:20,680
Whoa, w-what are you doing!

263
00:15:20,820 --> 00:15:24,700
M-meter! Oh no, it's b-broken!
You broke it! Ah!

264
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:28,340
You broke the ratings meter?

265
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:30,480
I'm terribly sorry.

266
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:35,740
Well, this guy wouldn't confess what the device was,
so I got carried away.

267
00:15:35,740 --> 00:15:37,540
I'm sorry.

268
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:41,900
But I just can't believe it.

269
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:46,480
Is this thing really chosen as a
sample household for the ratings?

270
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:47,720
It's true.

271
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:51,340
Isn't there some kind of proof or something?

272
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:53,600
Like a list of the sample households?

273
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:56,120
I'm not allowed to show you that.

274
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:58,180
Don't worry about that.

275
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:00,400
The president here is
Nakagawa Keiichi, right?

276
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:04,020
I'm his superior at the police,
and a relative.

277
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:05,620
I'm no outsider at all.

278
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:07,940
R-really?

279
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:10,540
I'm not trying to do anything malicious.

280
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:12,660
Just a quick peek, just a quick look.

281
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:15,840
Just a moment, please.

282
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
Kanto, Kanto, and...

283
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,120
Listen, Hojo.

284
00:16:21,170 --> 00:16:24,680
When the list comes,
memorize everyone's names and addresses.

285
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:28,720
You're going to put your
memory to use. Got it?

286
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:29,680
Yes!

287
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:31,180
Here it is.

288
00:16:31,660 --> 00:16:32,940
Sorry, thanks.

289
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:37,000
Hey, Hojo. Is your name on there?

290
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:51,120
Next is 2-36-6 Nishikokubunji, Kokubunji City,
Tokyo, the green one on the back.

291
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:52,760
Good, that makes 600.

292
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:58,380
So the households with pins in them
are the ones with ratings meters?

293
00:16:58,380 --> 00:17:01,300
Yes, that's right.
So what exactly are you...

294
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:09,460
From now on, we'll be the gods of ratings,
freely manipulating the ratings however we please!

295
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:24,480
Has your grandmother lived
on this land the whole time?

296
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:26,100
Eighty years.

297
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:28,280
Found it, the ratings meter!

298
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:31,800
From that position,
it should be at this angle.

299
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:34,680
Come on, Gonji, press channel 8.

300
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:35,300
Ugh.

301
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:38,520
The Ravant!

302
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:39,980
Yes, it worked!

303
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:43,780
Right, let's move on to the next one.

304
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:44,500
Ugh.

305
00:17:57,440 --> 00:17:59,340
Producer Suwa, you have a phone call.

306
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:01,840
Hey, what's up, Ryo-chan?

307
00:18:02,500 --> 00:18:05,000
If this is about that ratings business,
spare me.

308
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:09,040
But if it can bring in 30%,
that's a different story, right?

309
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:11,740
Wait, what's that supposed to mean?

310
00:18:11,740 --> 00:18:15,556
The ratings for GO! GO!
on the gateball show airing tonight,

311
00:18:15,606 --> 00:18:17,120
I'm looking forward to it.

312
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:44,000
Now the winning rate
is entirely in my hands.

313
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:47,060
Gateball GO! GO! is about to begin.

314
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:50,740
Gateball, go go!

315
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:54,140
Alright, everyone, switch to channel 8!

316
00:18:54,140 --> 00:19:02,820
Gateball, go go!

317
00:19:02,820 --> 00:19:07,260
Gateball, go go!

318
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:09,960
Gateball, go go!

319
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:15,200
Gateball, go go!

320
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:16,580
Gateball, go go!

321
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:17,040
Gateball, go go!

322
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:17,600
Eek!

323
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:20,500
It's the Stay edition!

324
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:23,100
We're getting switched back to a
different channel again and again.

325
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:25,140
Let's make sure it does.

326
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,720
18 minutes and 40 seconds
until the broadcast ends.

327
00:19:31,720 --> 00:19:33,940
Household number 256 has been switched back.

328
00:19:33,940 --> 00:19:35,180
Next, 413.

329
00:19:35,180 --> 00:19:36,280
218

330
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:36,840
13

331
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:38,120
109

332
00:19:38,120 --> 00:19:39,460
215

333
00:19:39,460 --> 00:19:40,880
444

334
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:42,100
329

335
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:44,840
GO! GO! Gateball, see you next week!

336
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:02,900
GO! GO! on the gateball broadcast hit 65.3%.

337
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:05,080
Suwa-san, you have a call.

338
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:06,840
Hello?

339
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:11,540
Did you see it? The GO!
GO! gateball ratings.

340
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:14,540
So that was your doing after all.

341
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:16,880
Now you know what I'm capable of.

342
00:20:16,880 --> 00:20:20,340
When the day comes,
I can get you even higher ratings.

343
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:24,440
How about it?
100,000 yen per percentage point.

344
00:20:24,440 --> 00:20:27,880
No, but that would go
against the ethics of a TV man.

345
00:20:27,880 --> 00:20:32,220
Weren't you the main producer of
Bakushō Television Yarō right now?

346
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:33,960
Right.

347
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:36,640
Maybe I'll boost the
ratings of the rival show.

348
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:36,960
What?

349
00:20:38,340 --> 00:20:40,740
Look forward to the next broadcast.

350
00:20:40,740 --> 00:20:56,740
Uuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu

351
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:59,000
Quick, switch it to something other than 8!

352
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,620
If it stays on the same channel for four seconds,
it gets recorded.

353
00:21:02,620 --> 00:21:05,280
Switch to a different channel
so it doesn't get recorded.

354
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:07,360
Target ratings: 0%.

355
00:21:07,960 --> 00:21:09,600
To those foolish TV station people,

356
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:11,840
I'll show them how terrifying I am.

357
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:18,260
Suwa-san, you have a call.

358
00:21:19,280 --> 00:21:19,920
Hello?

359
00:21:19,920 --> 00:21:23,560
I hear they've decided to
cancel *Bakushō TV Yarō*

360
00:21:23,560 --> 00:21:25,020
As for you,

361
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:30,840
Well, I hope your next
show gets good ratings

362
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:37,080
If your next show got cancelled,
I wonder what would happen to you?

363
00:21:37,600 --> 00:21:42,280
Getting demoted or something?
What are you going to do, Suwa-chan?

364
00:21:42,280 --> 00:21:48,380
Fine, you want good ratings, right?
200,000 yen per 1%.

365
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:55,780
If 100,000 yen is too little for you, that's fine.
In exchange, I'll boost the rival show's ratings.

366
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:01,280
All right,
I'll get you the highest ratings in Japan.

367
00:22:01,280 --> 00:22:02,860
Just you wait.

368
00:22:06,180 --> 00:22:09,000
Listen,
the highest ratings in Japan until now

369
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:12,760
was 82.4% for the 14th Kōhaku Uta Gassen.

370
00:22:12,760 --> 00:22:14,940
We're going to break that record

371
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:17,300
with Gekiretsu Television
Yarō starting today!

372
00:22:17,300 --> 00:22:18,060
Yeah.

373
00:22:19,020 --> 00:22:21,680
Gekiretsu Television Yarō is about to begin.

374
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:25,120
Gekiretsu Television Yarō!

375
00:22:26,820 --> 00:22:28,760
Alright, all channels to 8!

376
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:30,280
8.

377
00:22:34,180 --> 00:22:36,260
It keeps getting switched back!

378
00:22:36,260 --> 00:22:38,980
Don't let it go back to another
channel for even a second!

379
00:22:59,140 --> 00:23:01,900
We're apparently receiving
some shocking news here.

380
00:23:01,900 --> 00:23:03,380
Suwa-san, on the scene.

381
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:05,120
Yes, this is the scene.

382
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:09,160
This is the room of the man who boosted
the ratings and stirred up the whole country.

383
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:14,020
This is the man.
Everyone, please take a look.

384
00:23:14,020 --> 00:23:14,460
Yes.

385
00:23:19,740 --> 00:23:21,600
Yesterday's "Totsugeki TV Yarō."

386
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:25,060
They say it recorded the highest
ratings in television history.

387
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:27,880
Apparently, the people who were
chosen as the sample households

388
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:32,120
wanted to see the culprit who had
been secretly changing their channels.

389
00:23:32,120 --> 00:23:34,960
He actually got the highest ratings himself.

390
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:37,540
I bet Ryo-chan is thrilled.

391
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,080
He really is a troublemaker, isn't he.

392
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:44,760
Well, the TV people were overjoyed. As long as the numbers come in,
they don't care.

393
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:50,660
Damn that guy! He betrayed us!

394
00:23:53,900 --> 00:23:54,680
Hold on a second.

395
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:58,380
Wait, didn't they say there'd be a
bath scene with Reiko later in the show?

396
00:23:58,380 --> 00:23:59,860
What are you talking about?

397
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:02,460
Damn it, I got tricked!

398
00:24:03,140 --> 00:24:03,540
What?

399
00:24:15,300 --> 00:24:18,040
As if someone is calling

400
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:20,580
An afternoon after the rain

401
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:27,200
Walking while looking back,
holding Mom's hand

402
00:24:28,220 --> 00:24:34,420
That strange feeling I had back then

403
00:24:35,160 --> 00:24:39,620
So on the tiles of the shopping arcade

404
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:45,380
I tried walking only on the black ones

405
00:24:47,880 --> 00:24:53,540
Everyone was a child once,
but everyone has forgotten

406
00:24:54,660 --> 00:25:01,020
Even the self-important politicians
and the aunties with blue eyeshadow

407
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:06,580
Try turning at that corner

408
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:11,800
You can meet everyone, everyone

409
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:12,780
Don't go

410
00:25:12,780 --> 00:25:16,720
That place is Kameari

411
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,260
Well, surely it's Kameari

412
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:24,940
Everyone, come to our town

413
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:26,540
Thank you.

414
00:25:28,300 --> 00:25:34,380
What? A drinking ban?
That's too harsh, Chief.

415
00:25:34,380 --> 00:25:39,560
Sure, I was wrong to get drunk at the topping-out ceremony and wreck the house,
but

416
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:43,560
but what on earth would be
left of me if you took my sake?

417
00:25:43,560 --> 00:25:46,440
Next time: Ryo-san's drinking ban order!

418
00:25:46,440 --> 00:25:47,580
See you.

419
00:25:48,660 --> 00:25:50,940
That place is Kameari.

420
00:25:51,900 --> 00:25:54,680
Well, surely it's Kameari.

421
00:25:55,500 --> 00:25:59,260
Everyone, come on over to our town!

422
00:25:59,260 --> 00:26:01,240
What a good feeling.

423
00:26:01,240 --> 00:26:03,140
Stay tuned for next time.

